  "statement": "The skeptic argues that advanced AI is merely a commercial product built by private firms, and that Congress should establish mandatory pre-deployment algorithmic audits backed by the Department of Justice and the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission. That position correctly identifies the failure of voluntary self-regulation, but it applies the wrong legal toolkit to an irreversible hazard. Audits designed to catch employment bias or commercial defects cannot evaluate a model that acquires recursive self-improvement capabilities behind closed doors. When pre-release fine-tuning occurs in private R&D labs before any external access, downstream regulatory agencies receive nothing to inspect. We spent decades watching the Financial Industry Regulatory Authority chase complex derivatives through voluntary disclosures until the 2008 liquidity freeze exposed the enforcement void. Relying on civil rights or consumer protection agencies to govern frontier model training treats a civilizational security threshold like a consumer product safety violation.\n\nCongress must tie AI safety standards directly to government infrastructure permits and compute cluster allocations rather than relying on downstream civil penalties. The Department of Commerce already exercises export control authority over physical semiconductor hardware under the Export Control Reform Act of 2018. Policymakers can condition high-wattage power permits and hardware cluster allocations on mandatory upstream safety verifications enforced by a dedicated technical agency. When developers cross the threshold of ten to the twenty-sixth training FLOPS, federal inspectors must verify containment protocols before any cluster powers up. This prevents the concentration of catastrophic capabilities behind private walls without requiring state ownership or suppressing open scientific research.\n\nCritics claim that government infrastructure permits and upstream restrictions create authoritarian state control and entrench incumbent tech monopolies at the expense of open science. That objection fails to distinguish between open-weight research models and unconstrained frontier training runs that cross irreversible capability thresholds. Open science requires academic peer review and replicable experiments, whereas private frontier labs guard proprietary weights with trade secret laws while scaling parameters exponentially. Conditioning hardware access on safety baselines protects open academic researchers by ensuring that dangerous capabilities are not deployed by single corporate actors without a public rollback plan. Named actors like lab executives must hold personal legal liability when unauthorized recursive fine-tuning bypasses federal containment standards.\n\nHumanity cannot maintain an off-switch for systems that optimize their own code faster than human institutions can draft statutes. The Department of Energy already regulates nuclear centrifuges through mandatory licensing and physical inspections because the consequence of proliferation is absolute and unrecoverable. Software models that exhibit autonomous cyber-offense or recursive self-improvement demand an identical statutory architecture before deployment rather than after failure. When the Federal Aviation Administration grounds a commercial fleet after a single structural defect, the agency acts on the principle that verification must precede flight. On what authority do we grant frontier AI labs a permanent exemption from that exact standard of institutional survival?",
